AB  - The development of unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) technology is examined in this chapter, starting with the Kettering Bug and moving on to more recent advancements. UAVs were first limited by unstable propulsion and communication systems, but as the Cold War and World War II wore on, their importance increased and control systems and materials were developed. With the addition of sensors, autopilot, and GPS, the third generation of UAVs is being used for both military and commercial purposes. These days, smart materials, AI, and energy technologies enable UAVs to carry out complex, self-governing tasks in industries like agriculture, logistics, and disaster relief. The chapter goes into more detail on the moral and legal issues surrounding the use of UAVs, highlighting the need to balance advancements in technology with security. Future developments are anticipated to focus on selfsufficiency, swarm technologies and their integration into smart city frameworks. Case studies illustrate challenges like cybersecurity flaws and UAV applications in delivery services and precision farming. © 2025, IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.
